# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# descriptions.phr - phrasebook for tell_about person descriptions.
#
# A description is assembled as:  opening -> body facts -> coda.
#  - :opening / :coda carry the speaker's attitude toward the LISTENER (you).
#  - body :sections are keyed on a FACT; a section is only used when the
#    subject actually has that fact, and a variant is skipped if any {fill}
#    it needs is unknown - so a phrasing can never assert what beliefs lack.
#
# Phrasings must be RELATION-AGNOSTIC and GENDER-AGNOSTIC: they describe a
# human reached through any kinship (mother, cousin, wife, friend, ...) and of
# either sex. Never write "mother" / "she" / "husband" literally - use {kin}
# and the gender fills. Pets, animals and objects get their own phrasebooks.
#
# Variant line:   "phrase text"            @ key:val key:val
#  - conditions (all must hold; val|val is an OR). keys:
#       class   lower | middle | upper        (the speaker's class)
#       you     likes | neutral | dislikes    (attitude toward the listener)
#       regard  loves | resents | fears | neutral  (toward the subject)
#       emotion happy | angry | sad | neutral
#       age     young | grown | old           (the subject's age band)
#       gender  male | female                 (the subject's sex)
#  - no @ line  = unconditional fallback.
#  - {fill}: name, kin, age:words, age:words+1, age:round, occupation, spouse,
#            interests, person (man/woman), consort (husband/wife),
#            pron / Pron / pron:gen / pron:obj, addr.
#  - [a|b|c]: pick one at random ([a|] may pick nothing).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

:opening
"My {kin} is {name}."
"My {kin}'s {name}."                                        @ class:lower
"{name}."                                                   @ you:dislikes
"{name}. My {kin}."                                         @ you:dislikes emotion:angry
"My {kin}? {name}."                                         @ regard:fears
"[Oh, |]that would be {name}, my {kin}."                    @ you:likes emotion:happy
"{name} - my {kin}, and a finer soul never lived."          @ you:likes regard:loves

:age
"{age:words}."
"{age:words}, maybe {age:words+1}."                         @ class:lower emotion:sad
"Near {age:round} now."                                     @ class:lower age:old
"Aged {age:words}."                                         @ class:upper
"{age:words}, and sharp as ever."                           @ age:old regard:loves
"Only {age:words} - {pron} has {pron:gen} whole life ahead." @ age:young regard:loves

:occupation
"{Pron} {occupation}."
"{Pron} {occupation}, and good at it too."                  @ regard:loves
"{Pron} {occupation} - respectable enough, they say."       @ regard:resents

:spouse
# The spouse belief is ongoing here, so every variant is present tense -
# "is married to", never the past-tense "married" / "wed".
"{Pron:gen} {consort} is {spouse}."
"{Pron} is married to {spouse}."
"{Pron}'s married to {spouse}."
"{Pron} is happily married to {spouse}."                    @ regard:loves

:interests
"{Pron} is fond of {interests}."
"{Pron} fills {pron:gen} days with {interests}."            @ regard:loves
"{Pron} cares for little but {interests}."                  @ regard:resents

:regard
"{Pron} is a good {person}, {addr}."                        @ regard:loves
"The kindest soul I know."                                  @ regard:loves
"Never met a {person} so fond of criticism."                @ regard:resents
"Warmth was never part of the bargain with {pron:obj}."     @ regard:resents emotion:sad
"One learns to mind one's manners around {pron:obj}."       @ regard:fears

:coda
"{Pron} has done nothing to deserve police attention."      @ you:dislikes
"I'd thank you not to trouble {pron:obj} unnecessarily."    @ you:dislikes class:middle|upper
"You'll likely get along splendidly."                       @ you:dislikes emotion:happy
"Ask {pron:obj} yourself, {addr} - {pron} will set you right."  @ you:likes
